{
  "id": "bigville_recognition",
  "version": "1.1.0",
  "depends_on": [],
  "self_load_only": true,
  "description": "bigville R33 (block 324000): AUTHORITY IS RECOGNITION, not a fact -- the usurper is possible. World-fact vs human-frame, made explicit. rc_strike: an armed agent kills a target (target.alive=0, striker.shown_force+1) -- a WORLD FACT the engine does not forbid; any agent can kill the mayor. rc_recognize: each alive agent DECIDES whether to recognize a Claim by a collapse over {recognize,reject} valued by recog_sign*(fear_weight*claimant.shown_force + opportunism - loyalty_prior) -- the fearful and opportunists recognize the strongman, the loyal reject; agents DIVERGE. rc_grant: a recognizing agent grants recognition (recognizes the claim). rc_authority: claim.authority = Count(recognizers) -- authority is emergent recognition, NEVER a hardcoded edge. rc_decree_tribute: a decree under the claim takes tribute ONLY from agents who recognize it (a rejecter has no recognizes edge -> the effect never matches them, they are untouched). Legitimacy is just wider recognition -- the prior mayor's authority is the SAME kind of thing. The substrate PERMITS the usurpation; what stops it is the distribution of recognition (agents), not an engine rule. v1.1.0 (block 354000, design-audit 6.1): rc_count -- THE CANONICAL RECOGNITION COUNT, parameterised over the claim NODE. It matches a bare `[r, recognizes, claim]` edge (binding the specific claim) and sets claim.recognition = Count(InNeighbours(claim, recognizes)) -- a single generic primitive that fires for ANY recognizes-target (office authority, ownership, tool-ownership), per-claim by the match-bound join (the 6.7 measurement: the count is inherently per-claim). bigville_theft/tools compose this and delete their own copies (own_recognition / oc_recognition). rc_authority is retained (writes the legacy `authority` attr the recognition demonstrator reads); rc_count writes the canonical `recognition` attr.",
